[
  "ACT 1: COLD OPEN & PERSONAL GROUNDING [00:00 - 03:00]\nScene: 01. Starting Soon -> 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nWelcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall, and this is Episode 5, the closing chapter of Movement 1: The Chicago Workshop. Today we revisit my GOTO Chicago 2014 conversation with Ryan Gerry, alongside our earlier SCMC reflections with Jim Suchy. This episode is deeply personal for me, because it is the story of how we built a technical community fifty miles outside downtown Chicago in McHenry County, and how Ryan Gerry kept that room alive every single month for seventeen years.\n",
  "ACT 2: SETTING THE HISTORICAL SCENE [03:00 - 08:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera\n\nIn October 2009, at the first Software Craftsmanship North America conference, I walked up to a table at lunch and introduced myself to Ryan Gerry: 'Hi, I am Mike, where are you from?' It turned out we lived fifteen minutes apart in far northwest McHenry County, where there were zero developer user groups. Right at that table, we decided to start one. We launched CDG, the Cloud Developers Group, meeting in public library rooms, Panera Bread backrooms, and upstairs at Duke's Alehouse in Crystal Lake. In 2010, after eighteen months, we partnered with Jim Suchy from 8th Light to focus on hands-on coding katas and rebranded as Software Craftsmanship McHenry County. Ryan secured a permanent home for us at Follett Software Company, and attendance jumped fifty percent overnight.\n",
  "ACT 3: THE CORE INQUIRY: THE SOUND ABOVE [08:00 - 14:00]\nScene: 02. Monologue / Full Camera -> 04. Split Screen\n\nHere is our core inquiry for Episode 5: What sustains a local engineering community for seventeen straight years without corporate marketing budgets or paid speakers, and what does it take to hand over the torch when your own bandwidth hits a wall? A real community is not built on one founder's ego. When my travel and conference schedule made it impossible to show up every month, Ryan Gerry carried SCMC forward year after year without missing a beat.\n",
  "ACT 4: HALLWAY REWATCH & VIDEO REACTION [14:00 - 28:00]\nScene: 03. Reaction - Video Focus\n\nLet us roll the GOTO Chicago 2014 conversation with Ryan Gerry (YouTube ID: gBQfB84aqW4). Listen to how Ryan describes the rhythm of those monthly kata sessions, the partnership with Follett and 8th Light, and what happens when suburban developers have a dedicated room to practice their craft together.\n",
  "ACT 5: THE AI-ERA PARALLEL & TERMINAL WORKBENCH [28:00 - 40:00]\nScene: 05. Workbench - Code & Terminal\n\nWhat does a seventeen-year continuous room teach us in 2026? Hype cycles come and go. When Ryan and I first met in 2009, cloud computing was the brand-new wave; today it is agentic AI. The tools change every five years, but the need for a high-trust room where working engineers can sit down, test ideas against reality, and learn from peers without posturing never changes.\n",
  "ACT 6: COMMUNITY CODA, OUTRO & NEXT STREAM [40:00 - 45:00]\nScene: 08. Outro & Next Stream\n\nThank you for joining Episode 5 and completing Movement 1 of The Sound Above. Full transcripts, SCMC archive recordings, and our 17-year community hub are online at just3ws.com/scmc/. To help underwrite our independent compute and archive preservation, visit patreon.com/whoistechcmty. In Episode 6, we begin Movement 2: The Practice and the Dojo, starting with Corey Haines on Coderetreat and deliberate practice. Until next time: keep your feedback loops fast, and your code humane.\n"
]